1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:01,820
April fifth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,140
Rent day.

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:05,780
Meera counts out five thousand rupees and

4
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,140
hands it to the landlord.

5
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:10,349
Last time, every rupee that left the galla

6
00:00:10,509 --> 00:00:13,389
came back as something — a cart, some tea

7
00:00:13,469 --> 00:00:14,029
and sugar.

8
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:15,509
But this time?

9
00:00:15,789 --> 00:00:17,389
No new asset arrives.

10
00:00:17,469 --> 00:00:18,989
No debt gets smaller.

11
00:00:19,069 --> 00:00:20,269
Nothing comes back.

12
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:22,902
The scale tips.

13
00:00:23,182 --> 00:00:25,182
So what's on the other side?

14
00:00:31,391 --> 00:00:33,551
First, last time's answers.

15
00:00:33,871 --> 00:00:34,471
One.

16
00:00:34,751 --> 00:00:37,511
After Gopal's delivery, the equation reads:

17
00:00:37,711 --> 00:00:39,551
eighty-eight thousand equals thirty-eight

18
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:42,031
thousand, plus fifty thousand.

19
00:00:42,711 --> 00:00:43,231
Two.

20
00:00:43,511 --> 00:00:45,511
Buying the cart for cash doesn't move the

21
00:00:45,591 --> 00:00:46,311
scale.

22
00:00:46,471 --> 00:00:49,031
Cash goes down, the cart comes in — a swap

23
00:00:49,071 --> 00:00:50,151
on the left.

24
00:00:50,831 --> 00:00:51,391
Three.

25
00:00:51,631 --> 00:00:53,871
Paying back part of Ravi Mama's loan?

26
00:00:54,111 --> 00:00:57,071
Cash goes down, and so does what Meera owes.

27
00:00:57,191 --> 00:00:59,231
Both sides go down together, and the scale

28
00:00:59,311 --> 00:01:00,351
stays level.

29
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:02,931
And paying back a loan is not an expense

30
00:01:03,251 --> 00:01:04,931
— the debt just got smaller.

31
00:01:05,979 --> 00:01:09,099
And Checkpoint one — Aman's samosa cart.

32
00:01:09,419 --> 00:01:09,739
One.

33
00:01:10,219 --> 00:01:12,819
Aman puts in twenty thousand of his savings.

34
00:01:13,019 --> 00:01:14,699
Cash up, capital up.

35
00:01:14,779 --> 00:01:17,339
Twenty thousand equals zero, plus twenty

36
00:01:17,419 --> 00:01:18,379
thousand.

37
00:01:18,959 --> 00:01:19,599
Two.

38
00:01:19,679 --> 00:01:21,679
A ten-thousand-rupee bank loan.

39
00:01:21,839 --> 00:01:23,679
Cash up, liability up.

40
00:01:23,759 --> 00:01:26,239
Thirty thousand equals ten thousand, plus

41
00:01:26,319 --> 00:01:27,359
twenty thousand.

42
00:01:27,824 --> 00:01:28,544
Three.

43
00:01:28,584 --> 00:01:30,944
A cart and fryer for eighteen thousand,

44
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:31,984
in cash.

45
00:01:32,024 --> 00:01:33,904
A swap on the left, so the totals don't

46
00:01:34,104 --> 00:01:34,784
change.

47
00:01:34,864 --> 00:01:35,584
Four.

48
00:01:35,664 --> 00:01:37,664
Three thousand rupees of potatoes, flour

49
00:01:37,744 --> 00:01:40,264
and oil, on credit from Sharma Kirana.

50
00:01:40,464 --> 00:01:43,184
Stock up, and what he owes goes up.

51
00:01:43,304 --> 00:01:45,664
Thirty-three thousand equals thirteen thousand,

52
00:01:45,824 --> 00:01:47,184
plus twenty thousand.

53
00:01:47,864 --> 00:01:49,064
Four out of four?

54
00:01:49,144 --> 00:01:50,904
Your scale never tipped either.

55
00:01:52,064 --> 00:01:53,584
So, back to rent day.

56
00:01:54,064 --> 00:01:54,904
Freeze.

57
00:01:55,184 --> 00:01:56,784
Which two things changed?

58
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:00,340
The first one is easy.

59
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,580
Cash went down by five thousand.

60
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:03,780
But the second?

61
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,500
Ravi Mama is still owed thirty thousand.

62
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:08,980
Gopal is still owed eight thousand.

63
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:10,900
Nobody's debt got smaller.

64
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:13,740
So the change has to land on the only thing

65
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:16,140
left — Meera's own share.

66
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:16,940
Equity.

67
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:19,394
Let's unfold Meera's tag.

68
00:02:19,634 --> 00:02:22,674
It opens into a card — the equity card —

69
00:02:22,754 --> 00:02:23,954
with two pockets.

70
00:02:24,694 --> 00:02:27,254
Capital: the fifty thousand Meera put in.

71
00:02:27,334 --> 00:02:29,254
And a new, empty pocket.

72
00:02:29,794 --> 00:02:31,914
The rent comes out of that new pocket.

73
00:02:31,994 --> 00:02:34,794
For now, it reads minus five thousand.

74
00:02:35,347 --> 00:02:37,667
Eighty-three thousand equals thirty-eight

75
00:02:37,827 --> 00:02:40,707
thousand, plus forty-five thousand.

76
00:02:40,747 --> 00:02:41,907
The scale settles.

77
00:02:42,987 --> 00:02:45,227
Money that is used up to run the business,

78
00:02:45,307 --> 00:02:48,027
gone for good, is called an expense.

79
00:02:48,867 --> 00:02:51,427
An expense shrinks the owner's share.

80
00:02:52,844 --> 00:02:54,244
Now the fun part.

81
00:02:54,444 --> 00:02:56,244
Meera opens for business.

82
00:02:56,444 --> 00:02:58,764
Raju, her new helper, keeps the glasses

83
00:02:58,844 --> 00:02:59,404
moving.

84
00:02:59,644 --> 00:03:02,844
Two weeks of chai — glass after glass after

85
00:03:03,004 --> 00:03:03,724
glass.

86
00:03:04,082 --> 00:03:06,802
By April fifteenth, the galla has collected

87
00:03:06,882 --> 00:03:09,282
eighteen thousand rupees from sales.

88
00:03:09,442 --> 00:03:11,682
Cash goes up by eighteen thousand.

89
00:03:11,762 --> 00:03:13,122
Meera, your turn.

90
00:03:13,202 --> 00:03:14,482
Where's the second change?

91
00:03:16,531 --> 00:03:18,691
She didn't borrow it, so there's no new

92
00:03:18,771 --> 00:03:19,491
debt.

93
00:03:19,571 --> 00:03:22,291
She didn't add her savings, so capital stays

94
00:03:22,371 --> 00:03:23,651
at fifty thousand.

95
00:03:23,811 --> 00:03:24,851
She earned it.

96
00:03:24,971 --> 00:03:27,411
So it goes into the new pocket — right beside

97
00:03:27,491 --> 00:03:28,211
the rent.

98
00:03:28,681 --> 00:03:31,401
One lakh one thousand equals thirty-eight

99
00:03:31,561 --> 00:03:34,441
thousand, plus sixty-three thousand.

100
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:36,961
Money the business earns by selling what

101
00:03:37,041 --> 00:03:39,361
it sells is called revenue.

102
00:03:40,041 --> 00:03:42,361
Revenue grows the owner's share.

103
00:03:43,616 --> 00:03:45,856
Now peek inside that new pocket.

104
00:03:46,096 --> 00:03:48,336
Eighteen thousand came in as revenue.

105
00:03:48,416 --> 00:03:50,816
Five thousand went out as an expense.

106
00:03:50,896 --> 00:03:51,776
What's left?

107
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,140
Thirteen thousand rupees.

108
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:59,460
That's profit, so far — revenue, minus expenses.

109
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,080
Now the pocket has a name.

110
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:04,380
And look where it lives.

111
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:07,620
Inside equity, right next to Meera's capital.

112
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:11,040
Ravi Mama and Gopal are owed fixed amounts.

113
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,560
But every rupee of profit belongs to the

114
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:14,280
owner.

115
00:04:14,831 --> 00:04:17,351
So our equation gets a little longer.

116
00:04:17,631 --> 00:04:20,591
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

117
00:04:20,711 --> 00:04:22,911
plus revenue, minus expenses.

118
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:24,631
Same scale.

119
00:04:24,791 --> 00:04:26,951
We just looked inside Meera's share.

120
00:04:28,198 --> 00:04:30,838
Now, here's the trap most new owners fall

121
00:04:30,998 --> 00:04:31,238
into.

122
00:04:31,798 --> 00:04:35,158
Money left the galla, so it must be an expense.

123
00:04:35,681 --> 00:04:36,201
Nope.

124
00:04:36,401 --> 00:04:37,441
Remember the cart?

125
00:04:37,681 --> 00:04:39,521
Thirty-six thousand rupees left the galla

126
00:04:39,601 --> 00:04:40,561
for that too.

127
00:04:40,641 --> 00:04:42,161
So ask one question.

128
00:04:42,801 --> 00:04:44,881
Is it used up — gone for good?

129
00:04:45,121 --> 00:04:45,841
The rent?

130
00:04:46,081 --> 00:04:46,601
Yes.

131
00:04:46,801 --> 00:04:49,521
April is paid for, and May will need more.

132
00:04:49,601 --> 00:04:50,321
The cart?

133
00:04:50,561 --> 00:04:51,121
No.

134
00:04:51,161 --> 00:04:53,041
It's still standing right there, making

135
00:04:53,121 --> 00:04:53,841
chai.

136
00:04:54,461 --> 00:04:55,621
That's an asset.

137
00:04:56,869 --> 00:04:57,709
Your turn.

138
00:04:57,989 --> 00:05:00,229
Just imagine this — it's not in Meera's

139
00:05:00,309 --> 00:05:00,909
books.

140
00:05:01,189 --> 00:05:03,349
Meera pays three hundred rupees for a gas

141
00:05:03,429 --> 00:05:04,109
refill.

142
00:05:04,389 --> 00:05:06,149
Which two things changed?

143
00:05:09,316 --> 00:05:11,636
Cash, down three hundred.

144
00:05:11,676 --> 00:05:14,356
And the profit pocket, down three hundred.

145
00:05:14,836 --> 00:05:17,476
The gas burns away making chai — it's used

146
00:05:17,556 --> 00:05:17,796
up.

147
00:05:18,116 --> 00:05:19,076
An expense.

148
00:05:19,642 --> 00:05:20,482
One more.

149
00:05:20,762 --> 00:05:23,402
Meera buys a second kettle for fifteen hundred

150
00:05:23,482 --> 00:05:24,242
rupees.

151
00:05:24,522 --> 00:05:26,202
Which two things changed?

152
00:05:29,372 --> 00:05:31,772
Cash, down fifteen hundred.

153
00:05:31,812 --> 00:05:34,492
And Equipment, up fifteen hundred — a swap

154
00:05:34,532 --> 00:05:35,452
on the left.

155
00:05:35,932 --> 00:05:37,692
The profit pocket doesn't move.

156
00:05:37,732 --> 00:05:40,012
The kettle stays, and keeps making chai

157
00:05:40,092 --> 00:05:41,212
for years.

158
00:05:41,292 --> 00:05:42,252
An asset.

159
00:05:43,507 --> 00:05:46,267
So — Meera's share has two pockets.

160
00:05:46,467 --> 00:05:48,507
Revenue fills the profit pocket.

161
00:05:48,707 --> 00:05:50,387
Expenses drain it.

162
00:05:50,427 --> 00:05:52,227
And profit is the difference.

163
00:05:53,359 --> 00:05:54,079
Your turn.

164
00:05:54,239 --> 00:05:55,239
Three quick ones.

165
00:05:55,439 --> 00:05:55,919
One.

166
00:05:56,159 --> 00:05:58,159
Is the thirty-six-thousand-rupee cart an

167
00:05:58,239 --> 00:05:59,079
expense?

168
00:05:59,359 --> 00:05:59,999
Two.

169
00:06:00,079 --> 00:06:02,239
What's Meera's profit after the rent and

170
00:06:02,319 --> 00:06:04,079
the first two weeks of sales?

171
00:06:04,159 --> 00:06:04,879
Three.

172
00:06:04,959 --> 00:06:06,879
Which side of the scale does profit live

173
00:06:06,959 --> 00:06:07,599
on?

174
00:06:07,839 --> 00:06:09,759
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

175
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:13,570
Next time, the office next door takes chai

176
00:06:13,650 --> 00:06:16,370
for the whole floor, every single day.

177
00:06:16,610 --> 00:06:17,610
Just one catch.

178
00:06:17,810 --> 00:06:18,850
They pay later.
